{"id":59,"course_id":15,"title":"Platform APIs, Backstage, and Templates","summary":"Expose infrastructure capabilities through stable interfaces.","sequence":3,"body":"## Mental model\nPlatform teams should publish versioned interfaces: templates, modules, charts, APIs, scorecards, and docs. Consumers need contracts, not tribal knowledge.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice defining template inputs, outputs, validation, and lifecycle support.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nSketch a self-service workflow for creating an EKS-backed service with Terraform, Helm, and ArgoCD.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-engineering-product-operating-model","course_category":"Platform Engineering","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":291,"term":"Platform Engineering Operating Model","context":"Platform Engineering","definition":"Learn how platform teams define golden paths, service ownership, paved-road APIs, SLOs, cost guardrails, and internal developer experience."},{"id":292,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":293,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":294,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":295,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":175,"lesson_id":59,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Platform APIs, Backstage, and Templates?","answer":"Expose infrastructure capabilities through stable interfaces.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":176,"lesson_id":59,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":177,"lesson_id":59,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
